s\npartial: Q4 FY26 segment revenues are [Actual], full-year segment splits\nare [Unconfirmed]. DXY and current credit spreads are [Unconfirmed].\nThese gaps widen the CI, not the point estimate.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1-1. Macro variable check (as of 2026-08-21/24)\n- 10Y Treasury: 4.74% (Aug 21), 4.65% (Aug 20); hovering at cycle highs,\n  drifting upward. [Actual \u2014 Federal Reserve H.15 release, 2026-08-24]\n- Real 10Y (TIPS): 2.44% [Actual \u2014 Fed H.15]; 10Y breakeven = 2.30%\n  [Arithmetic on Actual].\n- Fed funds (effective): 3.63% [Actual \u2014 Fed H.15]. Policy is ~100bp below\n  the prior 5.33\u20135.50% peak; regime is a paused/partial-easing stance, not\n  tight. 30Y at 5.31% [Actual] signals the market doubts the easing\n  durability.\n- DXY: [Unconfirmed]; consistent with a soft-ish dollar at ~99\u2013102\n  [Assumption].\n- Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed]; assumed near tights given 3.63% EFFR\n  [Assumption].\n- Inflation: breakeven 2.30% [Arithmetic], near target; but memory-chip\n  tariffs are a visible cost pressure for CSCO.\n- Economic cycle position: late expansion with an AI-capex boom; hyperscaler\n  capex projected at $805B (2026) and $1.116T (2027) for MSFT/AMZN/GOOGL/\n  META/ORCL [Estimate \u2014 Morgan Stanley via thetradable.com, 2026].\n\nStep 1-2. Regime probability distribution\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 30% \u2014 Fed has room to cut; AI capex\n  carries growth; but 10Y at 4.74% is not \"low rates.\"\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 35% \u2014 dominant: strong AI capex, 2.30%\n  breakevens, 30Y at 5.31%.\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 15% \u2014 tariff/memory-cost channel,\n  sticky long-end yields.\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 20% \u2014 the AI-capex cliff scenario;\n  hyperscaler projects are the single most fragile lever.\n- Total = 100%.\n\nStep 1-3. CSCO macro sensitivity (each -3 to +3)\n- Interest rate: -1 (corporate capex finance, but AI budgets are less\n  rate-elastic).\n- Dollar: +1 (~40% rev non-US; weak dollar helps; DXY assumed soft).\n- Liquidity: +2 (AI infra demand is balance-sheet/liquidity driven).\n- Economic cycle: +2 (hyperscaler capex is the swing factor).\n- Commodity: -1 (memory-chip pricing, tariffs).\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment\n- Expected impact = 0.30\u00d71.0 + 0.35\u00d71.4 + 0.15\u00d7(-0.5) + 0.20\u00d7(-0.3) \u2248 +0.6.\n- Verdict: mild tailwind through the reflation/goldilocks combination, but\n  the 20% recession scenario would hit CSCO's AI narrative directly.\n- Rule applied: Macro Net = neutral \u2192 no conviction step change.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates applicable to CSCO\n- Probability that a large hardware-incumbent sustains >10% revenue growth\n  for 5 consecutive years after an order surge: ~20\u201330% [Assumption,\n  framework base-rate library \u2014 applies to 1999 telecom, 2014 data-center\n  cycles].\n- Probability that a capex-driven supplier reverts to GDP-like growth\n  within 3 years of an order supercycle: ~60% [Estimate, framework].\n- Probability CSCO survives 5 years (not in distress): >95% [Assumption \u2014\n  $8.3B cash, $46.7B RPO, FCF positive; not applicable to the pre-revenue\n  base rate].\n- Large M&A (Splunk, $28B) achieving ROIC > WACC within 5 years: ~25\u201335%\n  [Estimate, framework base rate].\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate and justification\nMy base case has FY27 +15% but fades to +3.5% by FY31 \u2014 a 5-yr forward CAGR\nof ~7.9%. This is deliberately only modestly above the 20\u201330% sustain-base\nrate for the >10% growth tail. Three company-specific justifications for the\ndeviation above pure base rate: (1) AI backlog is contractually visible: FY26\nAI orders $9.3B vs FY27 AI revenue guide $7.5B \u2014 a built-in conversion floor\n[Actual]; (2) RPO at $46.7B (+7%) [Actual]; (3) design wins at four of top\nhyperscalers [Actual, company statements]. These justify the FY27\u2013FY29\nupslope, not a permanent re-rating.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base rate shock check\n- Bull case success probability (25%) exceeds the 20-30% sustain base rate\n  by only ~1.0x \u2014 no red flag, but no overrun either.\n- Market price, however, embeds a ~85% success path. That is >2.0x the base\n  rate \u2192 RED FLAG against the current price (not against the company).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences)\nCisco sells the physical and software fabric that connects enterprise and\ndata-center networks: switches, routers, optics, Silicon One silicon,\nsecurity (including Splunk), observability, collaboration and services.\nIt is the incumbent leader in enterprise networking, now pivoting hard\ntowards AI data-center Ethernet fabrics. The profit engine is shifting\nfrom one-time hardware to subscriptions/RPO, but hardware still drives\nthe current growth impulse.\n\nUnderstandability: Medium-High.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment + dynamics (0\u20133 each)\n- Intangible assets: 2 (brand, patents; little pricing power vs hyperscalers)\n- Switching costs: 3 (15M+ installed devices [Estimate via research brief];\n  mission-critical, high migration cost)\n- Network effects: 1 (ecosystem, not consumer network effects)\n- Cost advantage: 1 (Silicon One helps, but Nvidia/Arista have comparable\n  silicon economics)\n- Economies of scale: 2\n- Regulatory barriers: 2 (security/compliance in governments)\n- Data moat: 2 (Splunk, Cisco IQ telemetry, 8,600 customers [Actual call])\n- Total: 13/21 \u2192 Wide moat.\n- Moat direction per type: Switching costs Stable; Silicon One/data-center\n  share Widening in AI, but classic data-center Ethernet share Narrowing\n  vs Nvidia (IDC: Nvidia has overtaken Cisco in DC Ethernet switching\n  [Estimate via LinkedIn source]); Security / Splunk Widening. Net:\n  Stable with a widening tail and a narrowing flank.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification\nFast Grower (re-accelerated) with cyclical characteristics. What to watch\nfor the category: (a) sustainability of order growth, (b) share count\ndiscipline despite SBC, (c) one-time-customer concentration. All three are\nlive concerns here.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard\n- Revenue growth: FY21\u201325 CAGR +2.8% [Arithmetic on filed]; FY26 +12%\n  [Actual] \u2014 re-accelerating.\n- Operating margin: 12-yr median +25.3% [Actual]; latest +20.8% GAAP\n  [Actual, pack]; non-GAAP FY26 34.8% [Actual, call].\n- Net income: FY26 \u2248 $13.4B [Estimate \u2014 bridge: TTM NI $11.958B [Actual]\n  + Q4 FY26 est ~$4.0B [Estimate] \u2212 Q4 FY25 $2.550B [Actual]].\n- ROE: 27.3% [Aggregator]; ROIC \u224820% GAAP [Estimate].\n- FCF: FY25 $13.288B [Actual]; TTM $11.788B [Actual].\n- Net debt: total debt ~$26\u201328B [Actual/LT components + vendor scalar] vs\n  cash+ST inv $16.1B [Actual] \u2192 net debt \u2248 $10\u201312B [Estimate]; coverage\n  enormous (interest expense <$0.5B [Actual FY23]).\nJudgment: Excellent.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0\u20133 each)\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1 \u2014 repurchased at 22\u201330x GAAP earnings during the\n   rally (Q4 FY26 avg $111.53 [Actual call]).\n2. M&A track record: 2 \u2014 Splunk unproven; prior mid-size deals weak.\n3. Capex efficiency: 3 \u2014 low capex intensity, high incremental ROIC.\n4. Mix rationality: 2 \u2014 pays real dividends ($0.42/qtr, 15th straight year\n   raise [Actual call]); 99% of FCF returned [Actual call], but SBC offsets\n   part of it.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3 \u2014 beat-and-raised through FY26.\n- Insider: ownership 0.05% [Aggregator]; no 6-mo cluster data [Unconfirmed].\n- Total: 11/15 \u2192 Good.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2.5-1/2. R&D capitalization and SBC treatment\nR&D (~$8B/yr [Estimate]) and SBC are treated conservatively: SBC is a real\nexpense; R&D is expensed as incurred. Adjusted ROIC (capitalizing R&D,\n5-yr amortization): Adjusted NOPAT \u2248 GAAP OI $11.76B [Actual FY25] + R&D\n$8B \u00d7(1\u221220%) amortization \u2248 +$4.8B net; Adjusted invested capital \u2248 equity\n$46.8B [Actual] + unamortized R&D (~$30B [Estimate]) \u2248 $77B. Adjusted ROIC\n\u2248 21\u201324% [Estimate]. GAAP ROIC \u2248 20%; gap <5pp \u2014 no material distortion.\n\nStep 2.5-5/6. Owner earnings (true measure used in valuation)\nThe pack warns OCF adds back SBC. Owner FCF = OCF \u2212 SBC \u2212 capex.\n- FY25: $14.193B \u2212 $3.641B \u2212 $0.905B = $9.65B [Arithmetic on Actual].\n- FY26E: OCF \u2248 $16.5B [Estimate: TTM $13.025B + Q4 $5.4B [Actual] \u2212 prior\n  Q4] \u2212 SBC \u2248 $4.0B [Estimate] \u2212 capex \u2248 $1.3B [Estimate] = $11.2B\n  [Estimate].\nOwner FCF margin FY26 \u2248 17.7% [Estimate] \u2014 not the ~24% FCF margin that\nheadline OCF-based FCF implies.\n- This is the valuation base. [Estimate; the OCF/SBC/capex annual split for\n  FY26 is [Unconfirmed] in precise form; derived with stated components.]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3. VALUATION\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nLayer 0 selection: Archetype D (Product-Platform Hybrid: hardware +\ninstalled base/software monetization; RPO $46.7B [Actual], recurring\nsoftware revenue $6.2B/qtr [Actual]) with cyclical, capex-driven AI demand\ncharacteristics. Transition to Quality Compounder is <50% met (ROIC > WACC\nfor 3 years not yet proven post-Splunk; AI revenue still hardware-led).\nPer Step 0-3: 50\u201380% criteria met \u2192 Engine 3 (Hybrid) primary with Engine 2\n(cycle-adjusted) stress overlay; conviction \u22120.5 steps applied at the end.\n\nEngine 3 anchors (Q4 run-rates; full-year splits [Unconfirmed]):\n- Networking: $9.8B/qtr [+28% YoY, Actual]\n- Security: $2.2B/qtr [+14%, Actual]\n- Collaboration: $1.2B/qtr [+12%, Actual]\n- Observability: +6% [Actual]; Software total: $6.2B/qtr +11% [Actual]\n\nBecause full-year segment margins are [Unconfirmed], the core valuation is\ndone at total-company FCFF level, which is more defensible.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3-X. FORECAST PATH (base case)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nDecomposition anchors:\n- FY26 revenue $63.3B [Actual]; FY26 AI-infra revenue \u2248 $3.9B [Estimate],\n  implied by core +10% guide (core FY27 $65.3B = $72.8B \u2212 AI $7.5B\n  [Actual guides] \u21d2 FY26 core \u2248 $59.4B).\n- FY27 revenue $72.8B (midpoint of $72.2\u201373.4B) [Actual company guide].\n- FY28+: NOT guided \u2014 [Assumption] developed from (i) AI order backlog\n  $9.3B (4.5\u00d7 prior yr) [Actual], (ii) Morgan Stanley hyperscaler capex\n  +39% 2026\u21922027 [Estimate], (iii) framework base rate that hardware\n  supercycles revert, and (iv) IDC competitive data placing Nvidia ahead\n  in DC Ethernet [Estimate]. Growth fades to ~3.5% by FY31.\n\nGAAP net income path (uses guided GAAP EPS $4.00\u20134.06 FY27 [Actual]):\n| FY | Revenue | Rev growth | GAAP NI | NI margin | OCF | SBC | Capex | Owner FCF | FCFF* |\n|----|---------|-----------|---------|-----------|-----|-----|-------|-----------|-------|\n| 26E| $63.3B  | +12% [A]  | $13.4B [E] | 21.2% | $16.5B [E] | $4.0B [E] | $1.3B [E] | $11.2B [E] | $12.4B [E] |\n| 27E| $72.8B  | +15% [A]  | $15.6B [E] | 21.4% | $19.3B [E] | $4.6B [E] | $1.6B [E] | $13.1B [E] | $14.3B [E] |\n| 28E| $80.4B  | +10% [A]  | $17.3B [E] | 21.5% | $21.3B [E] | $5.1B [E] | $1.7B [E] | $14.5B [E] | $15.7B [E] |\n| 29E| $85.4B  | +6.2% [A] | $18.4B [E] | 21.5% | $22.8B [E] | $5.6B [E] | $1.8B [E] | $15.4B [E] | $16.6B [E] |\n| 30E| $89.3B  | +4.6% [A] | $19.0B [E] | 21.3% | $23.8B [E] | $6.1B [E] | $1.9B [E] | $15.8B [E] | $17.0B [E] |\n| 31E| $92.5B  | +3.6% [A] | $19.4B [E] | 21.0% | $24.8B [E] | $6.7B [E] | $2.0B [E] | $16.1B [E] | $17.3B [E] |\n\n*FCFF = Owner FCF + after-tax interest ($1.2B/yr [Estimate]).\nRevenue FY27 is company guidance [Actual]; FY28\u201331 are author assumptions\n[A] calibrated to backlog + industry capex estimates + base rates.\nOwner-FCF margin by FY31 = 17.4% \u2014 consistent with 5-yr historical\nowner-FCF margin (OCF 27% minus SBC ~6% minus capex ~1.7% [Arithmetic]),\ni.e., no margin expansion beyond guided FY27.\n\nDiscount-rate derivation:\n- Rf = 4.74% (10Y UST, Aug 21 2026) [Actual \u2014 Fed H.15]\n- ERP = 4.75% [Assumption, range 4.5\u20135.0%]\n- Beta = 1.00 [Aggregator: vendor, window unstated]\n- CoE = 4.74% + 1.00 \u00d7 4.75% = 9.49% \u2192 9.5%\n- Kd pre-tax \u2248 5.0% [Assumption: A+/AA- spread]; tax 16% [Assumption \u2014\n  guided GAAP effective rate ~15%]; after-tax Kd = 4.2%\n- E/V = 97.3%, D/V = 2.7% (net debt ~$10B [Estimate] of $448B EV)\n- WACC = 0.973\u00d79.5% + 0.027\u00d74.2% = 9.36% \u2192 9.2% central used (end-year\n  discounting is conservative; 9.2% is the stated discount rate)\n- Sensitivity tested 8.5\u20139.8%.\n\nDCF output (base, end-year discounting):\nPV(FY27\u201331 FCFF) = 14.3\u00d70.916 + 15.7\u00d70.839 + 16.6\u00d70.768 + 17.0\u00d70.703 +\n17.3\u00d70.644 = $62.0B\nTerminal FCFF = 17.3 \u00d7 1.03 (g=3.0% [Assumption: \u2264 long-run nominal GDP,\nCisco grows at GDP with slight share gains, offset by AI-fabric share\nloss]) = $17.8B\nTerminal Value = 17.8 / (0.092 \u2212 0.03) = $287.1B; PV(TV) = $184.9B\nEV = $246.9B; + cash & ST investments $16.1B [Actual FY25; FY26E similar,\nUnconfirmed]; \u2212 total debt $26.0B [Estimate: filed LT+current $24.6B\n[Actual] vs vendor $28.1B [Aggregator]]\nEquity = $237.0B; \u00f7 3.941B shares [Actual] = $60.1/share \u2248 $60.\n\nCross-checks:\n- GAAP P/E at 15\u201318\u00d7 on FY28E EPS ~$4.50 (declining shares